Package: com.iosvcam.opencam
Name: iOS-VCAM OpenCam
Version: 0.6.29
Architecture: iphoneos-arm64e
Description: Open-source, editable virtual camera tweak. Runs in mediaserverd so it covers every capture client (stock Camera, TikTok, all apps). Pulls an RTMP stream on-device, decodes H264 via VideoToolbox, and overwrites the camera's OWN image buffer IN PLACE at BWNodeOutput -emitSampleBuffer: with a SINGLE GPU-accelerated VTPixelTransferSessionTransferImage (one pass, no per-frame pixel rotation, no CVPixelBuffer lock around the transfer), then passes the ORIGINAL sample buffer downstream - matching the closed vcamera's GPU sync discipline (render nodes stay pass-through; orientation is carried by the pipeline's existing metadata). Every landscape capture buffer is overwritten (landscape gate, no dedup, like the original); portrait buffers inherit OBS from the shared surface. The stock-Camera full-res still uses a separate 709 transfer session so the saved photo colour matches. Every client reads the same overwritten upstream IOSurface, so the app preview shows OBS. Fail-open everywhere (any error falls back to the real camera). AUDIO (as of 0.6.18): the microphone hook (VCamAudioMS.x + VCamAACDecoder) is compiled in - OBS audio is demuxed from the SAME RTMP stream (msg_type 8 -> AAC -> int16 PCM) and replaces the mic globally via a real-time-safe AudioUnitRender hook; fail-open and runtime-gated so it never affects the video path. 0.6.0: MILESTONE CLEANUP of the device-verified 0.5.9 stable - identical runtime behaviour, but the settled freeze-hunt A/B diagnostics (VCAM_VIDEO_DEDUP, VCAM_PRIVATE_DEDUP_KEY, VCAM_GPU_FENCE_FLUSH, VCAM_STAMP_DEST) and the dead opt-in photo-node path (VCAM_HOOK_PHOTO_NODES, always deadlocked the GPU) are removed, leaving only the live emit-overwrite path the original actually uses. Dark-scene saved-photo red remains at parity with the closed vcamera (rendered by the iOS16 deferredmediad Deep Fusion daemon, outside mediaserverd's reach - see VCAMERA_OPENVCAM_COMPLETE_REFERENCE.md). 0.6.1: fix the stock-Camera Photo-mode preview cycling (clear->stutter->gaussian-blur every ~3s at the ZSL/Live-Photo queue restart). Root cause (RE, engine @0x82650): the rotation session was not GPU-accelerated - only the transfer session was - so the rotated buffer feeding the GPU transfer was not GPU-synced. Photo mode is the only mode using the rotation path (portrait OBS -> landscape dst; TikTok/portrait dst skips it and always worked), so its preview updated only at the ~3s restart. Fix: set EnableGPUAcceleratedTransfer on the rotation session too, and use NSRecursiveLock for the rotate+transfer critical section (engine @0x823f8), both matching the closed vcamera. 0.6.2: the ~3s Photo-mode preview cycle is actually the portrait screen-preview buffer (1170x2532) being SKIPPED by the landscape-only gate and inheriting OBS by propagation from the shared landscape surface, which gaps at each ZSL/Live-Photo queue restart. 0.6.2 proved that overwriting the portrait DISPLAY buffer directly fences/wedges the GPU (OBS flashes -> freeze -> real camera), so the faithful landscape-only gate is kept. 0.6.3: fix the freeze instead by making the landscape overwrite land SYNCHRONOUSLY - set EnableGPUAcceleratedTransfer=false (VCAM_GPU_ACCEL=0) so the shared 2304x1728 IOSurface is written via CPU. The GPU-accelerated transfer was async and had not landed before the live-preview crop (1170x2532, downstream) read it, so the preview froze on the last frame and only refreshed at the ~3s ZSL re-read; a CPU transfer writes the bytes immediately so the preview crop always reads fresh pixels. 0.6.4: restore GPU-accelerated transfer (VCAM_GPU_ACCEL=1), faithful to the closed vcamera which GPU-accelerates all its transfer sessions and the rotation session. The earlier GPU cycling was on a GPU-fence-degraded environment (a preceding gate-off build wedged the IOSurface fence, which persists until reboot); on a clean/re-jailbroken environment the GPU path should behave like the original. Gate stays ON (no wedge). Fall back to 0.6.3 (VCAM_GPU_ACCEL=0, CPU transfer) only if the GPU path still cycles on a genuinely clean env. 0.6.5: fully faithful to the closed vcamera - collapse the separate 709 still transfer session into the SINGLE 601 session used for every buffer (preview, video, and the full-res still), matching the original which creates three identical 601 sessions and uses only one for all overwrites. Now the full transfer config equals the original: GPU-accel on (transfer + rotation), landscape gate on (getLive && w>=h), NSRecursiveLock, no dedup, CCW90 rotation, Trim scaling, 709 primaries/transfer + 601 matrix, one session. 0.6.6: fix a front-camera mirror regression - GPU-accelerating the ROTATION session (added in 0.6.1) composes FlipHorizontalOrientation and the CCW90 rotation in the opposite order, so the left/right mirror rendered as a 180-degree rotation. Force the rotation session to the CPU path (decoupled from VCAM_GPU_ACCEL, which still drives the GPU TRANSFER session); the rotation GPU-accel flag never helped the preview cycle anyway. Transfer stays GPU. 0.6.7: fix the front-camera mirror properly by matching the original's flip axis. The original mirrors with kVTPixelRotationPropertyKey_FlipVerticalOrientation (its ONLY imported flip key - it does not import FlipHorizontal); combined with the CCW90 rotation that is the correct left/right selfie mirror. OpenVCam had used FlipHorizontalOrientation, which is exactly 180 degrees off (FlipVertical = Rotate180 of FlipHorizontal), so the front camera rendered as a 180-degree rotation instead of a mirror. Switched to FlipVerticalOrientation to match the original. 0.6.8: revert the 0.6.4-0.6.7 orientation experiments back to 0.5.9's proven rotation/mirror (FlipHorizontalOrientation, rotation session left at VT defaults) and restore CPU transfer (VCAM_GPU_ACCEL=0). GPU transfer had broken the front mirror (180 degrees) and re-introduced the preview cycle on this device. NOTE: OpenVCam's rotation is an APPROXIMATION (aspect-based CCW90 + a hand-rolled front FlipHorizontal); it is NOT the original's method, which drives CW90/CCW90+FlipVertical from a video-orientation value (ivar 0x100). A faithful rotation replica is tracked as separate future work. 0.6.9: FAITHFUL rotation replica (RE 0x83e20-0x83e7c, disasm re-verified this session). CORRECTION of the 0.6.8 note: the engine ivar that drives the rotation is CAMERA POSITION (AVCaptureDevicePosition, set by the setter @0x82878), NOT a video-orientation value - the dispatch is a two-way cmp #1 on that position. The original's rotation session is therefore driven by which physical camera is active: back camera (position <= 1) -> kVTRotation_CW90 with NO flip; front camera (position == 2) -> kVTRotation_CCW90 + kVTPixelRotationPropertyKey_FlipVerticalOrientation (the selfie mirror). OpenVCam now replicates this exactly: rotation direction and flip are driven by gSourcePosition (from the -sourcePosition hook), replacing the previous approximation (a single CCW90 for both cameras + a hand-rolled front FlipHorizontal). The aspect compare (VCamAutoOrientDegrees) is retained ONLY to decide whether a quarter-turn is needed, which is faithful to the original's emit-side raw-vs-prerotated selection (RE 0x8477c-0x847b4: same src/dst orientation uses the raw frame, differing uses the rotated one). Emit-side single-lock reused-buffer transfer and CPU transfer (VCAM_GPU_ACCEL=0) are unchanged from the device-stable 0.6.3. NET CHANGE vs 0.6.8: front camera now mirrors correctly (CCW90 + FlipVertical, direction unchanged, only the flip axis corrected H->V); back camera rotation direction changes CCW90 -> CW90 to match the original (the one behaviour needing on-device confirmation - if the back camera preview turns 180 degrees off, swap the back branch back to 270). Removed the now-dead VCAM_AUTO_ORIENT_DIR / VCAM_FRONT_AUTOMIRROR knobs. 0.6.10: device result for 0.6.9 - the faithful back-camera CW90 came out 180 degrees (upside down) on switching to the rear camera. Root cause: the original's per-position rotation KEYS (back CW90 / front CCW90, RE 0x83e20) are defined against the original's OWN decoded frame; OpenVCam's H264 decoder delivers the OBS frame at a different origin, so the literal CW90 inverts the back camera here - the rotation DIRECTION is pipeline-relative and not literally copyable, unlike the FLIP AXIS (FlipVertical) which is. Fix: CCW90 for BOTH cameras (the device-correct, known-good pre-0.6.9 direction), with only the FRONT adding the FlipVertical selfie mirror. Net: front camera mirrors correctly (FlipVertical, the genuine 0.6.7-0.6.9 fix) AND the back camera is upright again. The FLIP-axis correction is kept faithful; only the (pipeline-relative) rotation direction reverts to CCW90. 0.6.11: STEP-BY-STEP FAITHFUL REPLICATION of the closed vcamera's decode + ingest path (per-step RE, no guessing). Decode: output pixel format changed 420v(video-range) -> 420f(full-range), the format the original's decoder hardcodes (RE 0x86f3c); removed the non-original IOSurfaceProperties dstAttrs key and the non-original software-decoder fallback (single VTDecompressionSessionCreate like RE 0x87238); session props (ThreadCount=2, RealTime) and colour tags (709/709/601/chroma-left) already matched. Ingest rotation MOVED FROM EMIT TO INGEST, faithful to setYUVSampleBuffer:(0x82ddc)->create90ImageBuffer:(0x829e0): the decode thread now stores the raw frame AND a CCW90 pre-rotated copy under ONE engine lock in VCamFrameStore; the emit hot path (modifyImageBuffer: equiv) does NO rotation, only picks raw-vs-prerotated by orientation + one transfer, holding the SAME lock (== the original's single ivar-0x18 lock across ingest and emit; two locks caused the 0.5.7 GPU-fence deadlock). DECISIVE ROTATION CORRECTION: the camera-overwrite pre-rotation is create90ImageBuffer: = kVTRotation_CCW90 with NO flip and NO position check, for BOTH cameras (RE 0x82b48). The earlier "back CW90 / front CCW90+FlipVertical by camera position" (0x83e20) is a DIFFERENT code path (the run loop, ivars 0xc8/0xd0) that the camera overwrite never reads and was mis-attributed; that mistake is what put CW90 on the back camera in 0.6.9 (-> upside down on device). So 0.6.11 removes the front-camera FlipVertical entirely (the original has none; the front selfie mirror is the downstream capture pipeline's job) and rotates CCW90 for both cameras, pre-rotated on ingest. create90 detail replicated exactly: fresh CVPixelBuffer per frame with swapped W/H and IOSurface { IOSurfacePreallocPages:0, IOSurfacePurgeWhenNotInUse:1 } (RE 0x82a74-0x82af0). GPU-accel stays at the device-stable CPU setting for now (original uses GPU; revisited in the GPU step). 0.6.12: GPU step — flip VCAM_GPU_ACCEL default 0->1 so BOTH the transfer session and the ingest rotation session use EnableGPUAcceleratedTransfer=YES, faithful to the closed vcamera (RE 0x82530 transfer + 0x82650 rotation). Earlier GPU attempts (0.6.5/0.6.6) froze the preview, but those ran on the non-faithful emit-side double-rotation architecture on a GPU-fence-poisoned environment; now rotation is a single CCW90 ingest pass under one engine lock (the original's discipline), so the GPU path should behave like the original. If the preview freezes on a clean device, fall back to the device-stable 0.6.11 (VCAM_GPU_ACCEL=0, CPU). 0.6.13: STAYS GPU (reverted the wrong 0.6.12->CPU idea) and FAITHFULLY REPLICATES THE DECODE-OUTPUT CHAIN that was previously skipped (user audit caught it; iron rule: original works with GPU => we must replicate whatever it does, not switch to CPU). The closed vcamera does NOT hand the raw decoded CVPixelBuffer to the engine; its Helper -imageBufferToSampleBuffer:timeStamp: (RE 0x77c30) WRAPS it into a CMSampleBuffer (CMVideoFormatDescriptionCreateForImageBuffer + CMSampleBufferCreateForImageBuffer) with a SYNTHETIC MONOTONIC timestamp (counter +20.0/frame, PTS=CMTimeMake((int64)(counter*600),600), timescale 600, duration/DTS invalid — NOT the RTMP DTS/CTS), and CVPixelBufferLockBaseAddress's the buffer across the wrap (forcing the GPU-decoded pixels to land — a sync point), then -outputFrame: (0x77d24) hands the CMSampleBuffer to setYUVSampleBuffer:. OpenVCam now replicates all of this: VCamDecodeOutput wraps + locks + synthetic-timestamps, VCamFrameStore -ingestSampleBuffer: stores the CMSampleBuffer as raw (== ivar 0x50) and CCW90-pre-rotates its image buffer (== ivar 0x70). This closes the decode-output divergence and is a strong candidate for the GPU photo->video preview cycling (the lock sync + steady timestamps fix the decode-session async scheduling across the mode switch). 0.6.14: strict-fidelity refinement (user audit) — VCamFrameStore -ingestSampleBuffer: now stores the raw frame via CMSampleBufferCreateCopy (an independent copy preserving timing + format description + attachments), EXACTLY like the original's setYUVSampleBuffer: (0x82e24), instead of a bare CFRetain. Completes the ivar-0x50 replication (0.6.13 had already switched the stored object from a bare CVPixelBuffer to the CMSampleBuffer; 0.6.14 makes it a copy, not a retain). 0.6.15: raw-vs-prerotated SELECT made faithful (user audit) — VCamAutoOrientDegrees now does the original's plain orientation-CLASS comparison on integer w/h (RE 0x8477c-0x847b4: rotated iff src landscape+dst portrait OR src portrait+dst landscape; square->raw), replacing OpenVCam's aspect-ratio log-distance minimiser. Same result for typical 16:9/9:16 but robust to atypical/near-square/transient buffer geometries (which can appear at the photo<->video mode switch) — also a candidate for the remaining GPU cycling. 0.6.16: batch of strict-faithful-replication fixes from a systematic code audit (each RE-verified against _orig_vcamera.dylib). (1) ingestSampleBuffer releases raw->NULL before CMSampleBufferCreateCopy (original's order, 0x82ddc) so raw+rotated never mismatch. (2) removed the non-original 500ms frame-age watchdog; emit gates only on _bLive + frame!=NULL (0x84498). (3) removed the self-invented FigCaptureSourceConfiguration sourcePosition hook (original doesn't hook it; its position feeds the run-loop path which is external-UI only). (4) decode INPUT gives NO sample timing (numTimings=0/NULL, 0x87440) instead of RTMP DTS/CTS. (5) decode input NAL wrapped without copy (kCFAllocatorNull, 0x873ec). (6) output synthetic timestamp is a per-instance ivar (== Helper ivar 0x30), not a process static. (7) always rebuild the decode session per sequence header (original 0x86f78; dropped self-invented reuse/backoff — RISK: re-created per GOP if the encoder re-sends SPS/PPS, watch err1100). (8) RTMP disconnect sets _bLive=NO and KEEPS the last frame (original clearCache 0x837e0 never clears 0x50/0x70; setLive: from the accept callback), no longer clears the frame or invalidates the decoder. (9) rotation session created + configured at engine init (0x82650), not lazily on the first frame. (10) transfer session likewise created + configured at engine init (0x82494), moved into VCamFrameStore (== ivar 0x88) — both engine sessions now eager. Still GPU (VCAM_GPU_ACCEL=1). 0.6.17: two more strict-faithful-replication audit fixes. (1) ZERO-COPY decode input: the RTMP payload was copied once (dataWithBytes NSData) before the no-copy block-buffer wrap, so the end-to-end memory chain wasn't identical. The original's -decode:size: (0x87390) takes a raw (void*, int) and wraps it no-copy (kCFAllocatorNull) straight from librtmp's reassembled contiguous m_body; decodeAccessUnit now takes (const void*, size_t) and wraps `body` directly (safe: the RTMP reader calls it synchronously and the VT decode is synchronous, so `body` outlives the decode). No behavioural change (pure memory management). (2) removed the dead mirror/rotation config knobs: VCamConfig read plist mirror/rotation but nothing on the overwrite path consumed them — leftovers from the pre-0.6.11 approximation era (same family as the removed VCAM_AUTO_ORIENT_DIR/VCAM_FRONT_AUTOMIRROR). The faithful overwrite rotates a fixed CCW90 (0x82b48) and never flips (front mirror is downstream), so the original has no such knob to drive. 0.6.18: UNIFIED AUDIO - OBS audio and video now come from ONE RTMP stream in ONE tweak. The RTMP reader already received audio tags (msg_type 8) but dropped them; now VCamRTMPSource demuxes the FLV AAC track, VCamAACDecoder decodes it (AudioConverter, configured from the stream's AudioSpecificConfig) to interleaved int16 PCM, and pushes it into the mediaserverd microphone-replacement ring that VCamAudioMS.x's AudioUnitRender hook consumes - so every capture app (stock Camera, TikTok) hears OBS audio. The hard real-time-safe consumer (lock-free SPSC ring + jitter buffer + render-thread memcpy/fade) is unchanged from the 0.8.0 media-active work; only the PRODUCER moved from the standalone :1936 PCM bridge to the shared RTMP stream (gAudioSourceRTMP=1; the legacy socket producer stays dormant so exactly one producer feeds the ring). No extra PC-side daemon or second tunnel. The AAC decode runs on the RTMP reader thread (off the render thread), audio is fail-open and runtime-gated (default on; disable flag / Enabled=false pref), and NOTHING in the known-good 0.6.17 video path changed - so audio can never wedge video. Original parity note: the closed vcamera does NOT replace the mic in mediaserverd (its audio is its own GPUImage+librtmp streaming), so this is OpenVCam's own faithful-in-spirit design, not a replication.
